[comment {-*- tcl -*- doctools manpage}]
[vset VERSION 0.1]
[manpage_begin canvas::track::circle n [vset VERSION]]
[see_also canvas::track::lines]
[keywords canvas]
[keywords crosshair]
[keywords tracking]
[moddesc   {Variations on a canvas}]
[titledesc {Show a circle following the mouse}]
[require Tcl 8.5]
[require Tk 8.5]
[require canvas::track::circle [opt [vset VERSION]]]
[description]

This package provides a utility class which shows a circle on a
canvas, centered on the mouse pointer while it is over the canvas,
e.g. to show the range of an action at the pointer.

[section {Class API}]

[list_begin definitions]
[comment {= = == === ===== ======== ============= =====================}]
[call [cmd ::canvas::track] [method circle] [arg objectName] [arg canvas]]

This, the class command, creates a new instance of the tracker, named
[arg objectName], and attaches it to the specified [arg canvas]
widget.  The tracker is enabled: the circle is shown when the pointer
enters the canvas, follows it and is removed when the pointer leaves
the canvas.  The radius is 10 pixels.

[para] The result of the command is the fully qualified name of the
instance command.

[list_end]

[section {Instance API}]

Instances of this class provide the following API:

[list_begin definitions]
[comment {= = == === ===== ======== ============= =====================}]
[call [cmd objectName] [method destroy]]

This method detaches the tracker from the canvas, removes the circle,
and destroys the instance.  It does not destroy the attached canvas.
Destroying the canvas does not destroy the instance.

[comment {= = == === ===== ======== ============= =====================}]
[call [cmd objectName] [method off]]
[call [cmd objectName] [method on]]

These methods disable and re-enable the following of the mouse.
While disabled, the circle is not shown when the pointer enters the
canvas and does not follow it; it can still be shown and moved with
the methods [method start] and [method move].  Note that
[method off] does not remove a circle already shown; use
[method done] for this.

[comment {= = == === ===== ======== ============= =====================}]
[call [cmd objectName] [method radius] [arg radius]]

This method sets the radius of the circle, in pixels.  A circle
currently shown is redrawn with the new radius.

[comment {= = == === ===== ======== ============= =====================}]
[call [cmd objectName] [method start] [arg x] [arg y]]

This method shows the circle centered on the point [arg x] [arg y],
given in window coordinates of the canvas (as from [const %x] and
[const %y] in a binding), removing a circle already shown.

[comment {= = == === ===== ======== ============= =====================}]
[call [cmd objectName] [method move] [arg x] [arg y]]

This method moves the circle shown to the new center [arg x] [arg y],
in window coordinates.  It does nothing if no circle is shown.

[comment {= = == === ===== ======== ============= =====================}]
[call [cmd objectName] [method done]]

This method removes the circle from the canvas.

[list_end]

[section EXAMPLE]

[example {
package require canvas::track::circle

canvas .c -width 300 -height 200
pack .c
canvas::track circle tracker .c
tracker radius 20
}]

[vset CATEGORY canvas]
